SM(541,{"n":"1 Corinthians 10 Conclusion","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/1 Corinthians 10 Conclusion.mp3","d":2654.4,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest, serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.88,"Okay, so we'll go ahead and get started."],[23.28,"And uh when we get started here, uh in just a minute we'll be picking up in St."],[28.64,"Paul's first epistle to the Corinthians, uh, chapter 10, verse 23."],[34.4,"And uh just to get us caught up a little, uh, or sort of smack in the middle of a discussion uh by Saint Paul, uh related to uh primarily things now surrounding the Eucharist, surrounding communion, surrounding uh the liturgy."],[59.16,"Although he's been talking about a lot of related related topics as well."],[65.8,"And part of what we'll be talking about as we go through tonight is sort of how these things fit together."],[71.24,"Because it could sort of seem like, well, he's talking about the Eucharist, and then he goes and talks about something else that doesn't seem to be related."],[77.24,"Then he goes back to talking about the Eucharist again."],[79.72,"Then he goes and talks about something."],[81.78,"It's like he's bouncing back and forth."],[83.78,"But there there is actually a flow to his to his argument that I think will will uh come out as we sort of go through it in sequence."],[97.38,"Um as I mentioned before, we're used to reading a lot of times we read the Bible sort of in these chunks."],[103.38,"And that could be helpful. because you have to know the individual pieces in order to understand the whole thing"],[110.62,"But also, in order to really see what a puzzle is about, you need the picture on the box."],[115.52,"Not just all the pieces."],[118.0,"So you have to have both."],[119.6,"You have to have an idea of the parts and an idea of the whole."],[122.8,"So we'll be getting uh, Lord willing, uh, some more of that, uh, the whole as we uh go through tonight."],[133.6,"And so uh last time, uh there was a lot, so but just to sort of briefly briefly catch us up, uh he uh St."],[144.96,"Paul was uh speaking to the Corinthians."],[148.48,"He had been talking to them about uh their need to separate themselves from the pagan worship that was still going on in the city, still going on in their families, still going on in their environs."],[162.28,"Um because we've we've gotten the firm impression that one of the big problems that St."],[168.76,"Paul is addressing in this letter is the fact that people thought they could kind of do both."],[174.77,"Or at least sort of keep one foot back in the worship of the city and those practices while the rest of them was in the church."],[185.49,"And uh St."],[186.53,"Paul has been trying to very forcefully make the point that uh you can't do that."],[191.51,"It's all it's with uh Christianity and the Church of Christ, it's all or nothing."],[196.93,"You can't just say, oh well, I'll cover my base over here, and then cover my base over there, and we'll keep all the gods happy."],[205.41,"That's not how it works."],[207.09,"Uh and so he uh in chapter 10 uh used the example of uh the Israelites uh in the old testament and talked about how uh in the the Exodus, particularly in the crossing of the of the uh sea, that they were in their own way baptized, they were in their own way, uh they received the Spirit in their own way. they ate spiritual food and drank spiritual drink in the wilderness, just as they received the Eucharist"],[244.65,"So St. Paul is setting up that you know, just like you, they had these experiences when they were made part of God's people."],[253.67,"But then you look at what happened to, in particular, that first generation that came out of Egypt, and in the wilderness they sinned, and the particular sins that St."],[263.27,"Paul brings up are idolatry and sexual immorality, and we talked a little bit how those are tied together by Saint Paul all the time."],[274.27,"And how because of that, because they fell into sin, even though they'd had these experiences and become part of God's people, they ended up following, falling in the wilderness and not receiving the promise."],[285.23,"And so St."],[285.87,"Paul is sort of giving a warning to the uh Christians in Corinth that the same can happen to them."],[294.59,"Yes, they've been baptized, yes, they've received the Holy Spirit, yes, they're receiving the Eucharist."],[300.91,"But if they fall into these same sins, they can likewise fall."],[305.15,"And so they need to separate themselves from these things."],[310.19,"And then after using that analogy, he talked about uh how uh essentially how sacrifice works."],[320.17,"He said, This is what sacrifice does, and he's reminding them of what it does because they understood this."],[326.9,"He said, the reason they have these rituals in Corinth that involve the whole city and involve the worship of the gods of the city, is that these are uh these festal meals that you're having, these sacrificial meals, are building a community."],[342.36,"They're establishing fellowship between you and other people and between the people of the city and those gods."],[348.36,"And St. Paul says pretty bluntly, those gods are demons."],[352.94,"And I don't want you having fellowship with demons."],[356.0,"This is bad."],[357.84,"And so uh this is not just a um a minor thing, this is not just a party, this is not like going to a Fourth of July parade that isn't a Christian holiday per se."],[370.48,"This is this is uh engaging in the worship of other gods when you do this"],[377.7,"Um he talks about he says, Look, this is what you're doing at a pagan altar, this is what we're doing in the Eucharist."],[386.75,"When we come and receive the Eucharist together, it's binding us together as a community."],[391.89,"And it's binding us together with Christ."],[394.31,"And establishing this communion with Christ."],[396.71,"You can't be a in communion with Christ and in communion with demons at the same time."],[402.33,"It doesn't work."],[403.37,"They're opposed to each other."],[405.85,"Um he even mentions, he says, remember, consider Israel according to the flesh."],[410.73,"He says, That's how the sacrifices at the temple work."],[413.21,"This is in the 50s AD"],[413.73,"Because at this time the temple's still standing."],[420.69,"Just as a side note here, because I don't think I pointed it out last time, you notice he doesn't say anything negative about the sacrifices and things that are going on at the temple in Jerusalem."],[433.81,"I didn't notice, but you're right."],[437.41,"He says, well, clearly, in these pagan temples, no."],[442.64,"You have to stay away."],[444.16,"He refers to, but doesn't say anything negative about the temple in Jerusalem."],[451.04,"And the reason for that is this."],[453.26,"He's making this argument that this is just how sacrifice works."],[457.12,"We have our sacrifice, the Eucharist, they have their pagan sacrifices."],[461.76,"In Jerusalem, they have their sacrifices."],[464.0,"And this is what sacrifice does."],[465.92,"It brings you into communion with each other and with these spiritual beings."],[471.6,"He says, uh it does the same thing."],[475.14,"This is just how sacrifice works."],[478.66,"What makes it good or bad, what makes it positive or negative, is who it's directed towards."],[484.5,"Who it is you're communing with."],[487.16,"Communion with demons, bad, communion with Christ, good."],[489.88,"Well, who are they communing with through the rituals at the temple?"],[495.4,"With Yahweh. With Yahweh, with the God of Israel."],[498.8,"Who is Jesus, according to St."],[500.92,"Paul. And so this is why he doesn't say you need to leave the temple."],[506.86,"This is why, as we saw in Acts, that gives a lot of people a lot of trouble."],[510.78,"In Acts, Christ ascends into heaven, the Holy Spirit comes, and the apostles just keep going to the temple."],[518.8,"And praying. Going to the temple and doing these things."],[523.12,"And remember at the end when St."],[524.88,"Paul got arrested, what St."],[527.84,"James directed it to do and what he was doing was he was going and making offerings at the temple for these people who were taking a Nazirite vow."],[535.04,"Oh, yes. And he was going and doing that to sort of show look, I'm not trying to overturn, you know, the what we call the Old Testament."],[542.41,"the I'm not trying to take the Hebrew Bible I'm not trying to take the Torah and throw it out window."],[550.34,"Uh and so that's why they were they they were uh they took the attitude that they did."],[557.48,"Because they knew who it was directed toward."],[560.3,"Now, things would then change later on."],[565.5,"Um obviously, AD 70, the temple gets destroyed, doesn't get rebuilt."],[572.64,"Which means now the non-Christian members of the Jewish community uh don't have a temple, which means they don't have a priesthood, they don't have sacrifices, they don't have any of that."],[585.76,"Uh, from the early Christians, of course, have those things"],[590.75,"The early Christians have the Eucharist."],[593.57,"And have churches and have a priesthood, appointed by the apostles."],[598.63,"So all of these things continue to exist."],[601.19,"So it's not an issue for them."],[602.71,"But that becomes a major issue."],[604.55,"And so over the course of about 400 years, what we now call Judaism."],[611.65,"Rabbinic Judaism is created as a religion."],[616.54,"And it's created not just as a different religion, but a lot of the things that go into its creation are directly based on a rejection of Christianity."],[626.94,"And you may not have heard this before, but this is actually not controversial among Jewish scholars"],[634.82,"If you ask Jewish scholars of the Talmud, which is written in the 5th and 6th centuries, and the Talmud is the Babylonian Talmud in particular, is the text, because the basic text for rabbinic Judaism in the 4th and 5th centuries, they will tell you that it was written as a response to Christianity."],[654.1,"Jewish scholars. How particular passages relate to Christianity and what in the Talmud goes back to before Christianity and what is new and all that everybody debates."],[668.3,"But nobody debates at all."],[669.98,"Everybody agrees it's a reaction."],[672.7,"It's a reaction to Christianity."],[675.02,"It's deliberately rejecting certain parts of the tradition of the Jewish people that Christianity had latched on to."],[684.62,"And so Rabbinic Judaism sort of lets go of them."],[688.62,"It says you can have them."],[715.1,"When you went to the temple in Jerusalem before it fell, you were not making any statement that Jesus was not the Messiah."],[724.86,"There was no right, there was none of that at all related to the temple."],[729.34,"In the fourth and fifth centuries, synagogues, you were making that statement."],[735.66,"Because their practice had changed."],[737.66,"And there are all kinds of things that popped up."],[739.5,"But one of the most major ones is that in the traditional Orthodox synagogue service at the end, there's a repeated prayer where it's affirmed four or five times in a row that God does not have a body."],[751.26,"Well, that's aimed at something obviously very particular."],[755.56,"And so if they're doing that as a Christian, you know, you you can't be party to that anymore."],[764.82,"Um, but at this point, at this point, there isn't that diametrical opposition."],[774.47,"Um, but that's I know I've mentioned this several times as we've been going through St."],[778.15,"Paul, it's going to continue to be important."],[780.63,"That one of the biggest mistakes we can make reading St."],[783.67,"Paul is that when he talks about when he talks about what's translated as the Jews, Judeans, or Judaism or uh the law and that kind of thing, is that we have in our heads later rabbinic Judaism and its views on things."],[800.37,"And we sort of assume that that's what it was in the first century, what St."],[804.37,"Paul's alive, and it's not."],[804.69,"It's not, it's very different"],[809.93,"Um it changes over time."],[813.05,"At least I got the impression when I was Presbyterian that Paul rejects the law."],[823.19,"Several times he says he doesn't."],[825.11,"Yes. And and I was aware of those, but it still sounded like he was rejecting the law most of the time."],[832.89,"Because because people take that reading of it."],[834.81,"Yeah. Uh huh."],[835.93,"Yeah. And because rabbinic Judaism in a lot of its elements is based on this rejection of Christianity, then yeah, when you compare that to St."],[847.45,"Paul, they don't match up."],[850.43,"Deliberately so they don't match up."],[853.89,"But that doesn't mean that that Saint Paul was setting himself apart from the religion of other Jewish people in his time who didn't believe all the same things."],[868.13,"Yeah. So that that that becomes a big problem."],[872.93,"That's basically why we had a Protestant Reformation."],[877.71,"Is is because of that mi uh uh m I obviously think misunderstanding that Luther understood, you know, uh Saint Paul to be reacting to in his case some kind of mixture of medieval Roman Catholicism and Orthodox Judaism sort of mushed together, you know."],[900.15,"He thought the Pharisees were, you know, that's what they and that's not who anyone was in the first century."],[909.35,"Um so yes, so Saint Paul is always, always completely rejecting any kind of pagan worship."],[920.57,"Any kind of idolatry, any kind of pagan worship."],[924.54,"But there is still not in the 50s, we talked about this in Acts too."],[931.02,"In the 50s, there is not a religion called Judaism and a religion called Christianity as these sort of two separate things."],[943.78,"There are Jewish people who have accepted Jesus as the Messiah, and there are Jewish people who have rejected Jesus as the Messiah."],[954.74,"And at this point, a lot of Jewish people who hadn't heard of Jesus yet."],[958.71,"Had made a decision either way."],[960.95,"And then there are Gentiles."],[963.77,"People from other nations who have accepted Jesus as the Messiah."],[969.05,"Those who haven't, and those who haven't heard about it yet."],[974.73,"And so there are at this point Christian synagogues."],[978.09,"We talked about this before, that there are synagogues where Christians are gathering on Saturdays on the Sabbath, and then Christians are having their own gatherings of just Christians to celebrate the Eucharist on Sunday."],[993.81,"Just people who have accepted Jesus as the Messiah."],[997.57,"Whereas on a Sabbath, it might be a mixture of people who have accepted Jesus and people who haven't."],[1006.45,"That's going to change at the beginning of the second century, when the Jewish community is going to basically throw the Christians out."],[1015.81,"It's going to say, you are no longer part of us if you accept Jesus as the Messiah."],[1021.17,"You're something else."],[1022.69,"And so that's when what's now the first part of our liturgy, with the reading of scripture and preaching and all of that, that used to be in the synagogue on the Sabbath gets moved and added to the celebration of the Eucharist."],[1038.65,"It's at the beginning of the second century."],[1041.85,"So Christians just start doing the whole thing together on Sunday because more efficient."],[1067.77,"Where we're going to begin. tonight"],[1069.81,"So, unless anybody had any leftover questions or comments or rumors or anecdotes, we'll go ahead and uh get started in Saint Paul's first epistle to the Corinthians chapter 10, verse uh 23."],[1092.37,"All things are lawful for me, but not all things are helpful."],[1096.37,"All things are lawful for me, but not all things edify."],[1102.85,"So Saint Paul here is returning to some themes he talked about before."],[1109.01,"Remember, he's been sort of giving various reasons why you shouldn't eat meat offered to idols and why you shouldn't participate."],[1114.89,"And the first reason he gave was he was sort of interacting."],[1120.41,"One of St."],[1120.97,"Paul's rhetorical devices in his letters is that he'll sort of interact with an imagined person."],[1128.03,"So he'll say, you may say, so someone may say, someone may object."],[1133.43,"And say this, here's my response."],[1136.0,"As sort of a way of writing and anticipating what they might be thinking."],[1141.12,"And so one of those was he said, Well, you'll say to me, an idol is nothing."],[1147.54,"Like this would be a reason someone would give him why it's okay for them to eat meat offered to idols."],[1151.8,"Idols' nothing. I don't worship them."],[1154.58,"I'm I don't I don't follow those gods."],[1157.34,"It's just meat."],[1158.38,"God made it."],[1160.4,"So, you know, when I eat it, it has nothing to do with worship."],[1164.88,"And St. Paul's response to that was, well, okay, I'm I won't argue with you about whether an idol is nothing."],[1171.53,"But even if you're right, then what happened what about the other person, the weaker person in the church, in the community, who sees you go and eat eat at an idol's temple or eat at one of these feasts."],[1186.01,"And so they decide, oh, it must be okay, and then they end up falling back into this pagan religion."],[1192.99,"So even if you think there's no problem with it, for this other person's sake, you should not do it."],[1200.89,"And then we saw last time in chapter 10 that he then goes on to say, well, yeah, and also you're communing with demons."],[1210.71,"So there's another reason."],[1212.01,"So he's giving multiple reasons."],[1213.37,"But now he's sort of returning to that first reason."],[1217.1,"He's saying, you know, all all things all things are lawful for me."],[1223.04,"I have freedom."],[1225.3,"I feel like I know I know something."],[1228.8,"And I don't need to be bound by these by these rules anymore."],[1234.45,"Um I'm and and this is something this is something we do. all the time"],[1241.78,"In terms of what we watch."],[1244.36,"What we read, what we look at, where we go, what we do."],[1248.6,"We're like, oh yeah, other people might have a problem with this."],[1251.5,"But not me, you know."],[1252.86,"Uh huh. I can watch this."],[1255.24,"This doesn't affect me."],[1257.56,"I could read this."],[1258.6,"I could spend my time doing this."],[1260.04,"It won't affect me."],[1262.26,"Well, let me ask you something."],[1264.18,"Sure. The all things are lawful for me."],[1268.74,"He doesn't mean that all things are."],[1271.38,"No. No, this is another one of those anticipated, this is probably a saying."],[1276.1,"Uh-huh. Oh, yeah"],[1277.87,"That right. That that, you know, if if you really understand, if you're one of the remember, he talked about knowledge at the very beginning."],[1281.47,"So a philosophy"],[1287.67,"This sounds like some Greek philosophy."],[1289.51,"Yeah. So right."],[1290.79,"So if you if you know sort of the way things work, then you don't have to follow all the little rules."],[1296.31,"The little rules are made for the people who can't really understand how things work."],[1302.21,"So yeah, the speed limit sign is there for people who aren't good judges of how fast they can drive and how well their car handles."],[1309.99,"But I I know that, and so I don't have to worry about it."],[1313.51,"That's true. Yeah, exactly."],[1315.78,"So that's a small example."],[1317.54,"But it it, you know, um, so it's not literally saying, yeah, you can still get a ticket for disobeying the speed limit, but it's an attitude toward uh these things."],[1327.62,"So someone might hear what St."],[1329.7,"Paul just said and say, well, yeah, okay, I can understand that for these, you know, these simple people."],[1335.76,"These farmers and stuff."],[1337.6,"Yeah, you know, if they go to one of those festivals or something, they might be sucked back in."],[1342.34,"But you know, I know the score here."],[1344.76,"Um it's gonna be fine."],[1348.12,"And so the point that St."],[1349.64,"Paul is making is uh even if you want to say that, that okay, it's fine for you to do it. it"],[1355.76,"It's not gonna affect you."],[1357.0,"Again, I'm not gonna argue that point."],[1358.44,"It's not that he's saying it really won't."],[1359.96,"He's just saying, I won't argue that point with you."],[1363.08,"But not all things are helpful and not all things edify or build up."],[1367.76,"Or help you grow"],[1367.82,"Or strengthen you."],[1370.38,"Remember, he's talked a lot about maturity, coming to maturity, as a Christian in the letter."],[1376.55,"So he says, okay, fine."],[1379.99,"All things are okay."],[1382.28,"But not all things are a positive good."],[1385.88,"Not all things are helpful."],[1387.56,"Not all things are good for you."],[1390.12,"You're just going around saying, well, nothing's bad for me."],[1393.64,"Okay, fine. But some things are good for you, and others aren't."],[1399.1,"And you have kind of have to acknowledge that."],[1401.58,"Nobody's going to go out and say, oh, all things are good for everything is good for me."],[1407.64,"Stubbing my toe on the wall is good for me."],[1409.72,"And so that's that's where St"],[1410.08,"All these things are good."],[1411.14,"Nobody's going to say that."],[1414.82,"Paul addresses that logic."],[1418.1,"And this applies to all those ways that we tend to use it."],[1422.51,"It's okay for me to watch this, even though I know it's not the best."],[1426.75,"It's okay for me to look at this stuff on the internet."],[1428.75,"It's okay for me to spend all this time on the internet."],[1430.75,"It's okay for me to."],[1431.8,"Because I know, you know, I'm not going to be affected by it."],[1436.9,"But even if we accept that, even if you accept that it's not going to have any immediate negative effect on you, which in many cases it will."],[1444.0,"Is it good for you?"],[1446.48,"Is this helping you?"],[1448.0,"Is this helping you grow?"],[1450.8,"Not just in terms of your relationship. with Christ, but just as a human"],[1457.4,"Are you going to be a better person in an hour for having done this?"],[1463.08,"Or are you going to look back and say, well, I go, wow, I wasted a lot of time yesterday."],[1467.94,"At best. I just wasted a bunch of time."],[1472.18,"And so St."],[1474.26,"Paul is, by saying this, sort of shifting the categories."],[1480.02,"We're not worried about, oh, whether this is bad or this is what's good."],[1486.5,"Let's do those things that are good, that are worthwhile."],[1493.78,"Let no one seek his own, but each one the other's well being."],[1497.06,"So he says, uh, and on top of that, this is additionally"],[1503.49,"And why are you only thinking about yourself, by the way?"],[1509.25,"This isn't going to be bad for me."],[1511.81,"What about everyone else around you?"],[1514.61,"What about your family?"],[1515.89,"What about your community?"],[1517.01,"What about your, you know, the other people in the church?"],[1520.13,"What about the other people in the city who are seeing how you act and live as a Christian?"],[1524.13,"Why aren't you thinking about them?"],[1527.97,"So not only should you be thinking about what is good and what builds up, and what is positive, but not just in terms of yourself."],[1535.95,"You should be thinking about what is good and what builds up and what is positive in terms of the other people around you."],[1540.75,"We talked about this a lot at the end of Romans"],[1544.47,"The importance of love as a positive thing, not just, oh, I don't wish anybody any harm."],[1551.43,"But actually loving people and displaying that."],[1554.73,"This is what he's saying."],[1555.77,"You need to think about what can I do that will help, you know, my parents, my children, my brothers and sisters, my family members, my wife, my husband, whoever."],[1568.52,"That's what we should be should be focusing on when we make all these decisions."],[1573.96,"He's going to go back to food offered to idols."],[1576.3,"But this, he's not just saying this about food offered to idols."],[1579.02,"This is in everything."],[1579.98,"This is a general principle to apply to all these issues."],[1587.22,"Eat whatever is sold in the meat market, asking no questions, for conscience' sake."],[1592.5,"For the earth is the Lord's and all its fullness."],[1596.02,"He says, so if it's just you, if it's just you."],[1600.87,"Go to the meat market."],[1602.31,"Don't ask where it came from, because you don't want to know, probably."],[1606.53,"Take it home, pray."],[1608.71,"Bless it. The earth is the Lord and the Lord's the fullness thereof, the real world and all those who dwell herein."],[1616.16,"Receive it, give thanks to God for it."],[1618.88,"Okay. If you're all by yourself."],[1625.45,"Verse 27 If any of those who do not believe invites you to dinner and you desire to go, eat whatever is set before you, asking no question for conscience' sake."],[1636.11,"So somebody invites you to their house."],[1638.8,"And they put food in front of you."],[1641.04,"Don't ask where it came from."],[1642.74,"If they're not Christians, if they're not believers."],[1647.06,"So they because you probably don't want to know."],[1650.35,"Give thanks for it."],[1652.13,"Eat it. Receive their receive their hospitality."],[1657.81,"But if anyone says to you, this was offered to idols, do not eat it."],[1662.53,"For the sake of the one who told you and for conscience' sake, for the earth is the Lord's and all its fullness."],[1668.29,"Notice he quotes the same verse to prove the opposite point"],[1676.5,"So what is he what does he mean by it in these two cases?"],[1682.84,"So it's it's clear what he's saying at first."],[1684.78,"He's saying if if you go to these, and they said it in front of you, you'd say, you know, we just finished offering this to Aphrodite."],[1693.19,"You say, oh."],[1696.95,"Time out, pass."],[1699.09,"I I can't eat it."],[1700.37,"And why does he say the first reason he lists is for the other person's sake?"],[1707.91,"Is to be a witness to the other person."],[1710.19,"That's number one."],[1711.31,"Number two is for your own conscience."],[1713.79,"Because he's applying what he just said."],[1716.85,"And if you want to argue with me about the conscience thing, okay, fine."],[1720.61,"You still have the other person."],[1723.11,"It's the reason not to do it."],[1724.71,"Okay, but now why does this verse, the first time he quoted the verse, it kind of made sense."],[1729.67,"Made intuitive sense."],[1731.0,"Because he's saying everything in the world, God created it, it belongs to him."],[1735.5,"So if he's giving it to you, give thanks for it and eat it."],[1739.18,"Now he's saying if it's been offered to an idol, don't eat it for the same reason."],[1745.74,"Because everything in the world belongs to God."],[1749.58,"Not clear. Well, and this is probably a little unfair, but where we left off at the very end last time."],[1762.35,"St. Paul talked about provoking the Lord to jealousy."],[1767.69,"And he was referring back with that jealousy language back to like the Ten Commandments."],[1772.49,"Lord your God is a jealous God."],[1774.41,"The commandments against idolatry."],[1776.81,"And against. And against worshiping pagan gods."],[1780.81,"So if this food has been offered to an idol, that means that the people who offered it have taken something that belongs to God and offered it to a demon."],[1792.41,"And given it to a demon."],[1795.69,"This is how St"],[1797.89,"Paul, remember, way back in Romans 1, when he talked about it, described what was happening in idolatry."],[1806.53,"That they were giving thanks and giving God's glory, the glory and thanksgiving that belonged to God for the good things they had in their life, and they were giving it to demons."],[1817.65,"They were giving it away to other gods."],[1823.41,"Rather than to the one who created it."],[1834.03,"is This this is meat that then needs to be returned to God who owns it."],[1840.95,"And we're not used to thinking this way in the modern world"],[1847.47,"But the Orthodox Church should help us start to think this way."],[1853.87,"We tend to think of material objects as neutral."],[1858.83,"They're just there."],[1861.47,"They're just there."],[1862.91,"Now, already as I say that, as I said that, I just pointed to my Bible."],[1868.43,"I'm willing to bet most of the people in this room, if not everybody in this room, doesn't treat a Bible the same way they treat other books."],[1876.83,"True. So that's already gotten through to you."],[1882.2,"That somehow this materi materialal obje objectct. is different than another"],[1886.8,"And it's not because this one's printed on holy paper from some sacred tree somewhere."],[1894.18,"Or that you know the ink came from, you know, none of that."],[1899.03,"It's what it is."],[1899.99,"It's that this this paper and this ink and these cardboard covers have been taken and made into the scriptures."],[1907.35,"The scriptures have been printed on them."],[1910.09,"And so this book has been taken and is different than all the other books."],[1916.49,"Another example I use a lot is back behind the altar, we have the chalice for the Eucharist."],[1924.09,"Back at home in the cupboard, I have my Star Trek 2, The Wrath of Khan. Commemorative Cup from I believe Burger King"],[1933.41,"The difference between those two is not that the chalice is made from metal ore mined on the holy mountain."],[1943.18,"Or fashioned through some sacred ritual."],[1948.03,"It's made out of the same kind of material stuff as my Star Trek Collector's Cup."],[1953.44,"But my Star Trek Collector's Cup gets used for all kinds of things."],[1958.02,"Mostly as a display item."],[1959.86,"But also, you put various beverages in it."],[1963.4,"Whereas the chalice is only used for one purpose."],[1966.28,"And it only holds one thing."],[1968.33,"The body and blood of Christ."],[1969.37,"ist The Euchar."],[1969.53,"And we treat it differently"],[1972.05,"That makes it different."],[1976.37,"Even though it's just a metal cup, you know, like all the others."],[1983.49,"So this is true."],[1985.33,"This is true."],[1986.45,"What we need to start doing, if we want to think the way St."],[1990.37,"Paul thought, is the whole world is that way."],[1995.97,"The whole world is that way."],[1998.85,"The whole world was created by God originally and was created good and belonged to Him."],[2006.53,"But a major part of what sin is us taking the things that God has made and belong to Him and using them for other things."],[2014.61,"That make them not good anymore and not holy anymore and not sacred anymore"],[2021.69,"And that doesn't just include material objects, that includes like our bodies, our minds."],[2027.31,"And these other things that God has created."],[2029.31,"But it also includes water."],[2033.79,"It includes our homes."],[2037.55,"It includes all of these things."],[2040.35,"And so for St."],[2042.99,"Paul and the other apostles, there isn't some sort of neutral ground somewhere that's like unclaimed."],[2051.57,"So for St."],[2052.69,"Paul, his whole mission, his whole mission as he sees it."],[2057.01,"We sort of led into this last time."],[2058.13,"His whole mission as he sees it is that there's all this territory outside of Judea"],[2065.21,"All these other nations."],[2067.21,"And through worshiping these demons and worshiping these pagan gods, they've given over themselves as slaves."],[2076.41,"They've given over their land, they've given over their families."],[2079.45,"They've given all these things over to these evil spiritual powers."],[2083.37,"And as he comes and proclaims the gospel, he's taking them back."],[2089.79,"For Christ. And it's not just the people."],[2095.15,"But it's the world around"],[2098.03,"This is why later in Christian history, Christians start going into pagan temples and reconsecrating them."],[2102.59,"This is why St"],[2107.53,"As churches. Cyril of Alexandria went to one of the most famous pagan Greek or Egyptian temples and brought the relics, the bodies of a bunch of martyrs there and had them buried there."],[2123.61,"Is that the serious idea?"],[2125.29,"Yeah. To take it from being a pagan temple that belonged to these evil spiritual powers and make it a place that now belongs to Christ and is now holy."],[2138.42,"And taking these things back."],[2140.66,"So this is done practically all the time in the Orthodox Church"],[2146.94,"This is why we go and bless people's houses."],[2152.3,"You come and you bless a house, you sprinkle the holy water."],[2157.42,"This house, whatever it's been used for, whatever has gone on in it over the past year, we're now taking it, we're dedicating it back to Christ."],[2169.66,"And I've told people, I had certain people at another church who thought that, you know, you only had to have a house blessed once."],[2177.98,"And so when they'd asked me why I was coming back again, I'd say, Well, did you sin in the house in the last year?"],[2184.46,"And if they said yes, I said, Well, then I gotta come bless it again."],[2187.6,"We gotta come and turn it back."],[2190.0,"This is what we're doing when we. bless water"],[2193.0,"We're not making it magic water so you can kill vampires with it."],[2196.94,"We're we're going and we're taking this water back from whatever it may have been used for."],[2204.62,"And we're returning it to what God created water to be cleansing, purifying, restoring of life and strength."],[2214.67,"And this is this is the mission of the church in the whole world."],[2220.51,"It's to take take the world back."],[2224.19,"Take the world back."],[2225.15,"And this is why, more than at least a lot of other churches, the Orthodox Church talks a lot more about ecology."],[2232.39,"And a lot more about the environment."],[2234.59,"In terms of caring for God's creation"],[2240.63,"Not exploiting it."],[2243.75,"Not ruining it, destroying it, using it for our own ends to make ourselves wealthy."],[2251.43,"But keeping it, protecting it, nurturing it, and uh the life in it."],[2259.59,"So that's why St."],[2260.95,"Paul can quote this in both ways."],[2264.47,"Everything belongs to God, and that means you should receive it with prayer and thanksgiving."],[2270.47,"And everything belongs to God, and therefore you shouldn't be offering it to anyone else."],[2278.71,"Because it belongs to. Him"],[2280.59,"So both are true because everything belongs to God."],[2289.87,"Verse 29. For why is my liberty judged by another man's conscience?"],[2301.47,"But if I partake with thanks, why am I evil spoken of for the food over which I give thanks?"],[2308.91,"So this is again somebody.'s objections"],[2315.09,"So he says, when I'm talking about conscience, I'm not focusing on yours, I'm focusing on the other person's, as we've already said."],[2320.86,"So he's saying, Well, one of you may say to me, Well, why should my freedom be taken away by this other person's opinions?"],[2331.67,"Because, you know, what does he know?"],[2333.67,"Maybe he's, you know, doesn't always talk."],[2337.27,"He's superstitious or something."],[2338.73,"You know, why why should I have to have to uh stop for him?"],[2344.65,"And then he says, look, if I if I receive it with thanks, I pray over it, I give thanks to God for it, why am I going to be judged?"],[2352.91,"You know, um when uh this is food that I gave thanks over."],[2363.63,"Verse 31, therefore, whether you eat or drink or whatever you do, do all to the glory of God, give no offense either to the Jews or to the Greeks or to the church of God, just as I also please all men in all things, not seeking my own profit, but the profit of many that they may be saved."],[2383.19,"He's coming back to that theme too."],[2384.63,"Remember, he said he tries to be all things to all men, so by all means he may save some."],[2390.89,"And so he's saying this is this is the goal."],[2394.74,"We're working for the salvation not primarily of ourselves, but working for the salvation of others."],[2403.13,"Our care and our love for others."],[2406.01,"And so if it's my freedom versus harming another person person. I should give up my freedom out of love for that"],[2415.73,"I should not do something that I'm free to do."],[2418.83,"In order to try and help and show show love to this person."],[2424.99,"This is a small question, but it's been nagging me."],[2429.07,"Um if they can rededicate a temple, was there any way that meat that had been offered to an idol could be re-sanctified?"],[2442.51,"Well, that's what that's what St."],[2444.11,"Paul is implying when he says, just take what's at the meat market and don't ask questions."],[2450.67,"And then pray over it and give thanks over."],[2453.73,"So the the problem isn't that the prayer doesn't work to bless the meat."],[2460.65,"That's not the problem."],[2462.33,"The problem is, again, the other person who sees you eating meat that came from an idol's temple."],[2468.25,"Okay. That's that's the issue."],[2471.85,"The issue is, yeah."],[2474.33,"Um, because yeah, you can make that argument."],[2476.83,"He's saying you could make that argument."],[2478.99,"It's not gonna be bad for me."],[2480.27,"It's like, okay, but number one, it's not just about you."],[2484.37,"And number two, there's nothing particularly good about it."],[2490.13,"In fact, you might be healthier eating vegetables than eating meat at a first century meat market."],[2498.57,"So yeah, there's no there's no virtue to it."],[2501.77,"I'm still not gonna eat vegetables."],[2503.98,"Um I mean, another just just quasi-obvious you know, contemporary example of this."],[2513.55,"I have no problem occasionally having an alcoholic beverage."],[2518.53,"But if I'm sitting down to eat dinner with somebody I know who's an alcoholic, I'm not gonna sit there and drink in front of them."],[2527.07,"Not because there's something wrong with me having a drink, not because having a drink is gonna harm me, not because there's something evil or sinful about it."],[2537.8,"But because it what it might do to this other person."],[2542.48,"And my love for him is more important than this is to me, because I can live without it."],[2549.92,"It's not doing me any any good to do it."],[2556.16,"But there's there are thousands."],[2559.42,"There are this happens with us a thousand times a day, and we don't stop to think about it."],[2565.76,"When we cut people off in traffic, when we jump into parking spaces, when we didn't watch you when we ignore someone, you know, who we know we shouldn't ignore, or when we right, that we just and we have. whatever our justification is"],[2584.28,"Well, I'm not responsible to help that person, or I'm not research."],[2591.08,"But you could have helped that person."],[2593.81,"And we tend to, as I've said a few times on our way through First Corinthians, think about our rights."],[2600.13,"And well, I don't have to do that, you know, rather than focusing on, as Saint Paul wants us to, what we can do to show love and to help and to help bring salvation to those around us in a positive way."],[2618.29,"And the the chapter break here is in a weird place because pretty clearly, as you could even see in the Orthodox Study Bible, chapter 11, verse 1 is very clear.ly the end of this section"],[2632.01,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2639.37,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2644.65,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":20.88,"lv":2,"h":"Picking up at 1 Corinthians 10:23","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Resuming <b>1 Cor 10:23</b> - \"smack in the middle\" of Paul's discussion</li><li class=\"d0\">Topic now: <b>Eucharist, communion, the liturgy</b> - plus many related topics</li><li class=\"d1\">seems to bounce: Eucharist → something else → Eucharist again</li><li class=\"d1\">but there is a <b>flow to the argument</b>; will come out in sequence</li><li class=\"d0\">Reading in chunks helps - need the pieces</li><li class=\"d1\">but to see the puzzle need <b>the picture on the box</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"need an idea of the parts <b>and</b> the whole\"</li></ul>"},{"t":133.6,"lv":3,"h":"Recap: separate from pagan worship","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul told Corinthians to <b>separate from pagan worship</b> still in city, families, environs</li><li class=\"d1\">big problem in letter: people thought they could <b>do both</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"keep one foot back\" in the city's worship</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's answer: Christianity is <b>all or nothing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">can't \"cover my base here, cover my base there, keep all the gods happy\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Ch. 10 example: <b>Israel in the Exodus</b></li><li class=\"d1\">crossing of the sea = their <b>baptism</b>; received Spirit; <b>spiritual food and drink</b> = their Eucharist</li><li class=\"d1\">yet first generation <b>fell in wilderness</b>: <b>idolatry and sexual immorality</b></li><li class=\"d1\">so baptized, Spirit, Eucharist - <b>can still fall</b> if same sins</li><li class=\"d1\">→ must separate</li></ul>"},{"t":310.19,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: how sacrifice works","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Sacrifice <b>builds community</b> - Corinthians understood this</li><li class=\"d1\">festal sacrificial meals establish <b>fellowship</b> between people, and city and gods</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: \"those gods are <b>demons</b>\" - don't want you in fellowship with demons</li><li class=\"d1\">not a minor thing - \"not like a Fourth of July parade\"</li><li class=\"d1\">it is <b>engaging in worship of other gods</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Same logic for the <b>Eucharist</b></li><li class=\"d1\">binds us as a community and <b>to Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>can't be in communion with Christ and demons</b> - opposed</li><li class=\"d0\">Also cites <b>Israel according to the flesh</b> - temple sacrifices work the same way</li><li class=\"d1\">this is the <b>50s AD</b>, temple still standing</li></ul>"},{"t":420.69,"lv":3,"h":"Why no criticism of the Jerusalem temple","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Side note: Paul says <b>nothing negative</b> about the temple in Jerusalem</li><li class=\"d1\">clear warning off <b>pagan temples</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Reason: sacrifice is just <b>how sacrifice works</b></li><li class=\"d1\">we have the Eucharist, they have pagan sacrifices, Jerusalem has its sacrifices</li><li class=\"d1\">all bring you into <b>communion</b> with each other and spiritual beings</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Good or bad = who it is directed toward</b></li><li class=\"d1\">demons - bad; Christ - good</li><li class=\"d1\">temple: communing with <b>Yahweh</b> = <b>Jesus</b> according to Paul</li><li class=\"d0\">Why in <b>Acts</b> apostles keep going to the temple and praying</li><li class=\"d1\">James had Paul make <b>offerings for Nazirite vow</b> men</li><li class=\"d1\">showing he's not throwing out the <b>Torah</b></li></ul>"},{"t":560.3,"lv":2,"h":"After AD 70: rabbinic Judaism arises","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>AD 70</b>: temple destroyed, never rebuilt</li><li class=\"d1\">non-Christian Jews: no temple, <b>no priesthood, no sacrifices</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christians still have <b>Eucharist, churches, priesthood</b> appointed by apostles</li><li class=\"d0\">Over ~<b>400 years</b> what we call <b>rabbinic Judaism</b> is created as a religion</li><li class=\"d1\">built partly on <b>rejection of Christianity</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"not controversial among <b>Jewish scholars</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Talmud</b> (5th-6th c., Babylonian) written as <b>response to Christianity</b></li><li class=\"d1\">details debated; <b>reaction</b> agreed by all</li><li class=\"d1\">deliberately lets go of parts of tradition Christianity latched on to: \"you can have them\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Before AD 70: going to the temple made <b>no statement</b> that Jesus wasn't Messiah</li><li class=\"d1\">4th-5th c. synagogue: it did - e.g. repeated prayer that <b>God does not have a body</b></li></ul>"},{"t":764.82,"lv":3,"h":"Don't read later Judaism into Paul","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Key for reading Paul: when he says <b>the Jews, Judeans, the law</b></li><li class=\"d1\">we picture <b>later rabbinic Judaism</b></li><li class=\"d1\">first century was <b>very different</b>; changes over time</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: (Presbyterian impression) Paul <b>rejects the law</b></li><li class=\"d1\">several times he says he doesn't</li><li class=\"d1\">rabbinic Judaism <b>deliberately doesn't match</b> Paul</li><li class=\"d1\">doesn't mean Paul set himself apart from other Jews of his time</li><li class=\"d0\">Basically <b>why we had the Reformation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Luther read Paul as reacting to medieval Roman Catholicism + \"Orthodox Judaism mushed together\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"that's not who anyone was in the first century\" - Pharisees</li></ul>"},{"t":909.35,"lv":2,"h":"The 50s: no Judaism and Christianity yet","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul <b>always</b> rejects pagan worship and idolatry</li><li class=\"d0\">In the 50s <b>no separate religions</b> called Judaism and Christianity</li><li class=\"d1\">Jews who accepted Jesus as Messiah; Jews who rejected; Jews who hadn't heard</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Gentiles</b> who accepted, who haven't, who haven't heard</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Christian synagogues</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christians gather <b>Saturday</b> at synagogue - mixed</li><li class=\"d1\">own gatherings on <b>Sunday</b> for Eucharist - only believers</li><li class=\"d0\">Change at start of <b>2nd century</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Jewish community <b>throws Christians out</b>: \"you're something else\"</li><li class=\"d1\">scripture reading and preaching move from synagogue into the <b>Eucharist on Sunday</b> = first part of our liturgy</li><li class=\"d1\">\"more efficient\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1092.37,"lv":2,"h":"1 Corinthians 10:23-24 - All things are lawful","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>10:23</b>: \"All things are lawful for me, but not all things are helpful... edify\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Returning to the themes of earlier reasons not to eat idol meat</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul's device: <b>imagined objector</b> - \"you may say...\"</li><li class=\"d0\">First objection: \"an idol is nothing\" - it's just meat, God made it</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: I won't argue - but what about the <b>weaker person</b> who sees you at the feast?</li><li class=\"d1\">falls back into paganism → even if no problem for you, <b>don't do it for their sake</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Ch. 10: second reason - <b>communing with demons</b></li><li class=\"d1\">now back to the first reason</li></ul>"},{"t":1217.1,"lv":3,"h":"\"I know the score\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">The attitude: \"I have <b>freedom</b>... I know something... don't need these rules\"</li><li class=\"d1\">we do this with <b>what we watch, read, look at, where we go</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"other people might have a problem - but not me\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"All things are lawful\" = probably a <b>saying</b> - Greek philosophy flavor</li><li class=\"d1\">if you <b>know how things work</b>, little rules are for people who can't</li><li class=\"d1\">image: <b>speed limit sign</b> is for people who can't judge their car - \"I don't have to worry\"</li><li class=\"d1\">can still get a ticket - it's an <b>attitude</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"I know the score\" - it's just for the simple farmers who might get sucked back</li></ul>"},{"t":1363.08,"lv":2,"h":"Not helpful, not edifying","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul won't argue the point - \"it's not going to affect you\"</li><li class=\"d1\">but <b>not all things are helpful</b>, not all <b>build up</b></li><li class=\"d1\">ties to his theme of <b>maturity</b> in the letter</li><li class=\"d0\">Nobody says everything is good for them: \"<b>stubbing my toe</b> on the wall is good for me\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Applies to internet, shows, how we spend time</li><li class=\"d1\">even if no <b>immediate negative effect</b> - \"which in many cases it will\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Is it good for you? Is it helping you grow</b> - just as a human?</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Will you be a better person in an hour... or say <b>I wasted a lot of time</b>?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul <b>shifts the categories</b>: not bad vs. okay but <b>what is good, worthwhile</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1493.78,"lv":2,"h":"10:24 - Seek the other's well being","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>10:24</b>: \"Let no one seek his own, but each one the other's well being\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Why only thinking about yourself?</li><li class=\"d1\">family, community, church, <b>people in the city watching how Christians live</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not only what builds <b>you</b> up - what builds up <b>others</b></li><li class=\"d1\">echo of end of <b>Romans</b>: <b>love</b> as positive, not just \"I wish nobody harm\"</li><li class=\"d1\">what can I do to help <b>parents, children, spouse</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Applies to <b>every decision</b> - not just food offered to idols</li><li class=\"d1\">\"general principle to apply to all these issues\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1587.22,"lv":2,"h":"10:25-28 - Meat market and dinner guests","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>10:25</b>: eat what's sold in the meat market, <b>asking no questions</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"for the earth is the Lord's and all its fullness\"</li><li class=\"d1\">don't ask where it came from - \"you probably don't want to know\"</li><li class=\"d1\">take it home, <b>pray, bless it</b>, give thanks</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>10:27</b>: unbeliever invites you - eat what's set before you, no questions</li><li class=\"d1\">receive their <b>hospitality</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>10:28</b>: if someone says \"this was offered to idols\" - <b>do not eat</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"we just finished offering this to <b>Aphrodite</b>\" → \"time out, pass\"</li><li class=\"d1\">reasons: <b>for the other person</b> (a witness) first; then <b>conscience</b></li><li class=\"d1\">argue conscience if you like - other person remains</li></ul>"},{"t":1676.5,"lv":3,"h":"The same verse used both ways","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul quotes <b>Ps 24:1</b> to prove the opposite point</li><li class=\"d0\">First use made intuitive sense - everything is God's, so give thanks</li><li class=\"d1\">now: if offered to idol, don't eat <b>for the same reason</b> - \"not clear\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Link back to <b>\"provoking the Lord to jealousy\"</b> and the commandments against idolatry</li><li class=\"d1\">offerers took something <b>belonging to God</b> and gave it to a <b>demon</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Same as <b>Romans 1</b>: idolatry = giving the <b>glory and thanksgiving</b> due God to demons</li><li class=\"d1\">giving away what the Creator gave</li><li class=\"d0\">So this meat needs to be <b>returned to God who owns it</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1840.95,"lv":2,"h":"Material things are not neutral","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">We think of material objects as <b>neutral</b> - Orthodox Church should change that</li><li class=\"d0\">Example: the <b>Bible</b> in his hand</li><li class=\"d1\">nobody treats it like other books</li><li class=\"d1\">not holy paper or sacred ink - it's been <b>made into the Scriptures</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Example: the <b>chalice</b> behind the altar</li><li class=\"d1\">vs. his <b>Star Trek II: Wrath of Khan</b> commemorative cup from Burger King</li><li class=\"d1\">both same kind of stuff; chalice used for <b>one purpose</b> - holds <b>body and blood of Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"we treat it differently, that makes it different\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1985.33,"lv":3,"h":"The whole world is that way","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">To think like Paul: <b>the whole world is that way</b></li><li class=\"d1\">created <b>good</b>, belonging to God</li><li class=\"d0\">Much of <b>sin</b> = taking what God made and <b>using it for other things</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not good, holy or sacred anymore</li><li class=\"d1\">includes <b>bodies, minds, water, homes</b></li><li class=\"d0\">For Paul no <b>neutral unclaimed ground</b></li><li class=\"d0\">His mission: territory outside Judea, nations</li><li class=\"d1\">through demon-worship they gave over <b>selves, land, families</b> as slaves</li><li class=\"d1\">gospel = <b>taking them back for Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not just the people - <b>the world around</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2098.03,"lv":3,"h":"Reconsecrating temples","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Later Christians <b>reconsecrate pagan temples</b> as churches</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>St Cyril of Alexandria</b> - famous temple; brought <b>relics of martyrs</b>, buried them there</li><li class=\"d1\">from belonging to evil powers → <b>belongs to Christ, holy</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2140.66,"lv":3,"h":"Blessing houses and water","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Done <b>all the time</b> in the Orthodox Church</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>House blessing</b>: sprinkle holy water, dedicate it back to Christ</li><li class=\"d1\">people thought once only - \"did you <b>sin in the house</b> in the last year? Then I gotta come bless it again\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Blessing water</b>: not \"magic water to kill vampires\"</li><li class=\"d1\">taking it back from whatever it was used for</li><li class=\"d1\">returning it to what God made water to be: <b>cleansing, purifying, restoring life and strength</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Mission of the church</b>: take the world back</li><li class=\"d1\">why Orthodoxy talks more about <b>ecology</b>, environment</li><li class=\"d1\">care for creation - not exploiting, ruining, getting wealthy; <b>keep, protect, nurture</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2259.59,"lv":3,"h":"Why Paul can quote it both ways","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Everything belongs to God →</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>receive it with prayer and thanksgiving</b></li><li class=\"d1\">and <b>don't offer it to anyone else</b> - \"it belongs to Him\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Both true because everything is God's</li></ul>"},{"t":2289.87,"lv":2,"h":"10:29-30 - Liberty and another's conscience","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>10:29</b>: \"Why is my liberty judged by another man's conscience?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Another objection: not <b>my</b> conscience but <b>the other person's</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"why should my freedom be taken away by his opinions? what does he know? superstitious\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>10:30</b>: \"if I partake with thanks, why am I evil spoken of?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">I pray over it, give thanks - why judged?</li></ul>"},{"t":2363.63,"lv":2,"h":"10:31-33 - Do all to the glory of God","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>10:31</b>: \"whether you eat or drink... do all to the <b>glory of God</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>10:32-33</b>: give no offense to Jews, Greeks, church of God</li><li class=\"d1\">\"just as I also please all men\" - recalls <b>all things to all men</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Goal: <b>salvation of others</b>, not primarily of ourselves</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>care and love for others</b></li><li class=\"d0\">My freedom vs. harming another → <b>give up the freedom</b> out of love</li></ul>"},{"t":2424.99,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: can idol meat be re-sanctified?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: if a temple can be rededicated, could meat offered to an idol be <b>re-sanctified</b>?</li><li class=\"d0\">That's what Paul implies - <b>pray over it and give thanks</b></li><li class=\"d1\">prayer works; problem is the <b>other person</b> who sees you</li><li class=\"d0\">Argument \"not bad for me\":</li><li class=\"d1\">number one, <b>not just about you</b></li><li class=\"d1\">number two, <b>no virtue</b> in it - \"might be healthier eating vegetables than meat at a first-century meat market\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2503.98,"lv":3,"h":"Examples: alcohol and daily small cases","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Contemporary example: an occasional <b>drink</b></li><li class=\"d1\">won't drink in front of a friend who is an <b>alcoholic</b></li><li class=\"d1\">nothing wrong with it, won't harm me - but <b>love for him matters more</b>; \"I can live without it\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Happens <b>a thousand times a day</b></li><li class=\"d1\">cutting people off in traffic, jumping into parking spaces, ignoring someone</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I'm not responsible to help that person\"</li><li class=\"d0\">We think about <b>our rights</b>: \"I don't have to do that\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: what can we do to <b>show love and bring salvation</b> to those around us</li></ul>"},{"t":2618.29,"lv":3,"h":"Chapter break placement","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Chapter break is in a <b>weird place</b></li><li class=\"d1\">even in the <b>Orthodox Study Bible</b>, <b>11:1</b> is clearly the end of this section</li></ul>"}]});
